        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Dong Son culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, centred on the Red River Delta; named after the village of Đông Sơn in Thanh Hóa province; related finds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, Malaysia and the Chinese provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>; sites include Đông Sơn and Núi Nấp.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1000 BCE – 1st century CE; it grew out of the Gò Mun culture and marks the culmination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>; its bronze drum culture disappears after the Han conquest of the Red River Plain.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Heger I bronze kettledrums decorated with a sun, frogs, warships and feather-headdressed warriors; lost-wax bronze casting; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming; water buffalo and pigs; fishing and sailing in long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>; bronze daggers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at Đông Sơn village from 1924; the bronze drums and analyses of their metal; early Chinese texts such as the Shiben; ancient DNA and skull studies; the history of the Vietic languages.</span></div>
        </div>
