    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Dong Son culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rice.jpg" alt="Rice" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Rice <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 3,000 years ago, the Dong Son people farmed rice by the Red River in northern Vietnam.</p><p>They kept water buffalo and pigs, and cooked sticky rice in a special pan.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They fished and sailed in long dugout canoes, boats made from hollowed-out tree trunks.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their metal workers were skilled at casting bronze, and they made great bronze drums.</p><p>One drum weighs 72 kilograms, and making it needed at least a tonne of copper ore, rock that holds the metal.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-son-drum.jpg" alt="Dong Son drum" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Dong Son drum <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The drums show a sun, frogs, warships and warriors wearing feather headdresses.</p><p>They were beaten in war, at feasts and when people mourned their dead.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture.jpg" alt="Đông Sơn culture" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Đông Sơn culture <span class="kid-tale-cr">Bình Giang, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>A Han Chinese army later conquered the Red River lands, and drum-making came to an end.</p><p>Today their drums, first dug up at the village of Dong Son, can be seen in Vietnam's museums.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Đông Sơn is an archaeological culture named after a village in Thanh Hóa province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, where bronze drums were first excavated in 1924; it centred on the Red River Delta.</li><li>Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, kept water buffalo and pigs, fished and sailed long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>, and cast bronze drums decorated with scenes of their world.</li><li>Who the Đông Sơn people were is debated: some link them to the Vietic ancestors of the Vietnamese, others see several peoples who shared one trading culture.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Cổ Loa drum weighs 72 kilograms, and casting it would have required the smelting of between 1 and 7 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ore.</p></aside>
    </section>

