    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Đông Sơn culture emerges from the Gò Mun culture in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese text Shiben gives the first literary mention of bronze drums</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1st century CE</span><span class="rf-text">After the defeat of the Trưng sisters and the Han general Ma Yuan’s conquest of the Red River Plain, the bronze drum culture disappears</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1010–1225</span><span class="rf-text">Scattered Đông Sơn bronzes are said to have been found again under the Lý dynasty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">The Austrian archaeologist Franz Heger, creator of a classification of bronze drums, turns to the drums</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">A fisherman, Nguyễn Văn Lắm, reports bronze finds to the École française d’Extrême-Orient, and excavations begin at Đông Sơn village</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970s</span><span class="rf-text">Finds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Isan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, show that bronze casting began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> first or developed there independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Đông Sơn culture, also called the Dongsonian or Lạc Việt culture, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> archaeological culture centred on the Red River Delta of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. It takes its name, “East Mountain”, from the village of Đông Sơn in Thanh Hóa province, where bronze drums were first excavated in 1924. Artefacts closely related to it date from about 1000 BCE to the 1st century CE.</p><p>The excavations began after a fisherman, Nguyễn Văn Lắm, told the École française d’Extrême-Orient in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> that he had come across large quantities of bronze objects. Scattered Đông Sơn bronzes are said to have been found as early as the Lý dynasty (1010–1225), and in 1898 they drew the attention of the Austrian archaeologist Franz Heger, who created a classification of bronze drums; the drums of Đông Sơn are his Heger I type. The first literary mention of bronze drums is in the Chinese text Shiben of the 3rd century BCE.</p><p>The culture grew out of the Gò Mun culture and marks the culmination of the Bronze Age and the opening stage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Scholars once traced bronze casting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but finds made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Isan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, in the 1970s showed that casting either began first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> or developed there independently. The Đông Sơn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">bronze industry</a> is therefore seen as local in origin rather than brought by migrants from China. Pottery and bronzes in a similar style from Thailand may be older still, possibly placing the culture’s ancestor in the region by the 4th or 5th millennium BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rice.jpg" alt="Rice" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oryza_sativa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-232.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Đông Sơn people were skilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers who kept water buffalo and pigs, fished, and sailed in long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>. The pestle, the oar and a pan for cooking sticky rice have been called the main characteristics of the culture, and the linguist Ferlus traced new words for them in the Vietic languages.</p><p>The bronze drums were used in war, when “the chief summons the warriors of the tribe by beating the drum”, in mourning and at feasts, and the scenes cast on them suggest that Đông Sơn leaders had access to bronze founders of great skill. A single large drum demanded a great deal of metal: the Cổ Loa drum, weighing 72 kilograms, would have needed between 1 and 7 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ore to be smelted.</p><p>Vietnamese historians attribute the culture to the early states of Văn Lang and Âu Lạc. The drums themselves picture daily life and ritual: drummers and other musicians, warriors, the processing of rice, birds, deer and war vessels. Musicians on several drums, including the Ngoc Lu drum, seem to play the khene, an instrument closely associated with Lao speakers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known directly of Đông Sơn beliefs. What can be said comes from the bronze drums, their decoration and the occasions on which they were used, and any reading of them is inference.</p><p>The decoration of the Heger I drums is the richest evidence. At the centre of the drumhead is a sun, and around it run rings of warships and of warriors in feather headdresses; models of stylised frogs sit on the drumhead. The frogs and the radiating sun recur on the larger, plainer Heger II drums too. What the sun, the frogs and the processions of boats and warriors meant to their makers is not recorded.</p><p>The drums’ uses point to ceremony. They sounded at mourning and at feasts as well as in war, which suggests that they had a part in rites for the dead and in communal celebrations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture.jpg" alt="Đông Sơn culture" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Đông Sơn culture</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dong_Son_axe.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bình Giang, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Đông Sơn people were skilled bronze casters, as the drums found widely through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> show. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> drums were made in large numbers in northern Vietnam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and parts of Yunnan. The founders used lost-wax casting, a technique based on Chinese practice, but the scenes on the drums are local: drummers and other musicians, warriors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> processing, birds, deer, war vessels and geometric designs.</p><p>Heger I drums carry intricate decoration around a central sun, with model frogs on the drumhead. Heger II drums, also called Li Lao drums, share the frogs and radiating sun but are much larger and decorated more plainly. According to the Lịch Sử Cổ Đại Việt Nam, the high lead content of Đông Sơn bronzes is what sets them apart from Chinese bronzes, and drums from Guangxi and Yunnan contain less lead than those of the Red River Plain.</p><p>Other bronzes show wider links: some Đông Sơn bronze daggers closely resemble Scytho-Siberian styles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Đông Sơn-style objects spread widely. Artefacts closely related to the culture, or believed to belong to it, have been found in Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and the Chinese provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, and its influence reached maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The lower lead content of drums from Guangxi and Yunnan suggests that drum-making spread outwards from the Red River Plain. The close relationship with the Dian culture of Yunnan has led some to see the two as variants of one culture: Alice Yao argues that both belong to a single material phenomenon represented by more than 250 bronze drums, and differ in name only because of the nationality of the archaeologists.</p><p>Who the Đông Sơn people were is debated. They spoke either Austroasiatic or Northern Tai languages, or were Austroasiatic speakers in close contact and mixing with Tai speakers. Ancient DNA shows that the Red River Delta’s earlier inhabitants were mainly Austroasiatic: people buried at Mán Bạc, a site of the Phùng Nguyên culture, are close to modern Austroasiatic speakers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Mlabri, while those of the Đông Sơn site of Núi Nấp show mixed ancestry. One study models the Núi Nấp people as about 65% Dushan-related and 35% northern East Asian-related.</p><p>Ferlus (2009) argued from the Vietic words for the pestle, oar and sticky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> pan, and from where Northern Vietic is spoken today, that the culture was Vietic and its people the direct ancestors of the Vietnamese. Historians including Keith W. Taylor, Nguyen Phuong and John D. Phan answer that there is no actual evidence for this; Phan notes that Đông Sơn-style artefacts occur as far afield as Malaysia and Fujian and probably belonged to several distinct peoples who traded with each other.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Đông Sơn culture ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> rule. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, its material evidence, such as the bronze drums, disappears at the time of the Trưng sisters’ defeat and the appearance of Han material culture, which suggests that the sisters led a failed indigenous uprising. After the Han general Ma Yuan conquered the Red River Plain, the bronze drum culture disappeared.</p><p>More than 120 Han brick tombs in the Red River region point to growing Chinese migration into the area, or to strong cultural influence of the newcomers on the local people, and the earliest Sino-Vietnamese loanwords date from around this time. The culture’s identity remains a live question: Vietnamese historians link it with the states of Văn Lang and Âu Lạc and some linguists with the ancestors of the Vietnamese, while other historians see no evidence for that link.</p><p>The drums remain its best-known legacy. The name Đông Sơn is still used by some as a catch-all term for bronze kettledrums across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, while others keep it for the Heger I type alone. Displays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-drum">Đông Sơn drum</a> surfaces can be seen in several of Vietnam’s cultural institutions.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Đông Sơn culture was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture centred on the Red River Delta of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from about 1000 BCE to the 1st century CE. Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, kept buffalo and pigs, fished and sailed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>, and cast bronze drums decorated with suns, frogs, warships and feathered warriors. Its drums and influence spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and it faded after the Han conquest of the Red River Plain.</em></p>

